                  <provenance type="#c"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_2">Peterborough,
                     Northamptonshire. Benedictine abbey of St Peter, St Paul, and St
                     Andrew</orgName><p>Scribble at end records obit of a monk 'monasterii
                     apostolorum Petri et Pauli de burgo'. Cf. 473. The last word 'burgo' is legible
                     by ultra-violet light.</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="#rejected"><orgName role="fmo" key="org_62">Bourne,
                     Lincolnshire, Augustinian abbey of St Peter and St Paul</orgName> <p>This
                     attribution was rejected in the second edition of Medieval Libraries of Great
                     Britain (1964), which did not change in the Supplement (1987).</p> <p>When the
                     corresponding Medieval Libraries of Great Britain index card was originally
                     written, it recorded the following information: "Scribbled on last page: <q>vº
                     kl Febr o' ffr' ioh' monachus sac et profess' monasterii Apostolorum petri et
                     Pauli de bur[..]</q>; M. R. James says 'Petri and Pauli' <hi rend="italic"
                     >might</hi> be St Augustine's Canterbury, but 'more probably perhaps Bourne as
                     in Lambeth 327 and 473.'"</p>Later additions to the index card show that Ker
                     made closer examination of the inscription under uv light ('b in the last word
                     legible by u.v. light') and ultimately connected the inscription to
                     Peterborough ('i.e. Peterborough').</provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners"><persName role="fmo"
                     >Sancroft</persName>.</provenance>
               </history>
